I've been trying out various route grouping ideas, and here's an API proposal for the first iteration of route grouping:

## Proposed API

```diff
// Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing.dll

namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;

public static class EndpointRouteBuilderExtensions
{
+     public static GroupRouteBuilder MapGroup(this IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, RoutePattern pattern);
+     public static GroupRouteBuilder MapGroup(this IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, string pattern);
}

namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing;

+ // REVIEW: Should this be interface? Have a public constructor?
+ public sealed class GroupRouteBuilder : IEndpointRouteBuilder, IEndpointConventionBuilder
+ {
+     public RoutePattern GroupPrefix { get; }
+     public void OfBuilder<T>(Action<T> configureBuilder) where T : IEndpointConventionBuilder;
+
+     IServiceProvider IEndpointRouteBuilder.ServiceProvider { get; }
+     IApplicationBuilder IEndpointRouteBuilder.CreateApplicationBuilder();
+     ICollection<EndpointDataSource> IEndpointRouteBuilder.DataSources { get; }
+     void IEndpointConventionBuilder.Add(Action<EndpointBuilder> convention);
+ }

// Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Abstractions.dll

namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;

+ public interface IGroupEndpointDataSource
+ {
+    IEnumerable<IEndpointConventionBuilder> ConventionBuilders { get; }
+ }
```

## Usage Examples

```C#
var app = WebApplication.Create(args);

var group = app.MapGroup("/todos");
group.MapGet("/", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.ToListAsync());
group.MapGet("/{id}", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.GetAsync(id));
group.MapPost("/", (Todo todo, TodoDb db) => db.AddAsync(todo));

var nestedGroup = group.MapGroup("/{org}");
nestedGroup.MapGet("/", (string org, TodoDb db) => db.Filter(todo => todo.Org == org).ToListAsync());
nestedGroup.RequireCors("AllowAll");

// Call extension methods tied to a specific type derived from IEndpointRouteBuilder
// This applies to all endpoints added to group and nestedGroup.
group.OfBuilder<RouteHandlerBuilder>(builder =>
{
    builder.WithTags("todos");
});

// Unfortunately, I cannot make the following work because WithTags would need to be called repeatedly.
//group.OfBuilder<RouteHandlerBuilder>().WithTags("todos");

// OfBuilder would also apply to the following even though it was called earlier.
// If we only applied conventions to already added enpoints OfBuilder could return T instead of take an Action<T>.
group.MapDelete("/{id}", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.DeleteAsync(id));
```

## Alternative Designs

### MapGroup takes a callback for adding endpoints and returns a GroupConventionBuilder

```C#
var app = WebApplication.Create(args);
app.MapGroup("/todos", group =>
{
    group.MapGet("/", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.ToListAsync());
    group.MapGet("/{id}", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.GetAsync(id));
    group.MapPost("/", (Todo todo, TodoDb db) => db.AddAsync(todo));

    // string org cannot be an argument to the configureGroup callback because that would require MapGet and other
    // IEndpointRouteBuilder extension methods to be repeatedly called fore every request.
    group.MapGroup("/{org}", nestedGroup =>
    {
        nestedGroup.MapGet("/", (string org, TodoDb db) => db.Filter(todo => todo.Org == org).ToListAsync());
    }).RequireCors("AllowAll");
}).OfBuilder<RouteHandlerBuilder>(builder =>
{
    builder.WithTags("todos");
});
```

I like that this provides structure to the code in this simple example, but the version of `MapGroup` that returns the `GroupRouteBuilder` in the main proposal is more flexible. It doesn't require capturing the `IEndpointRouteBuilder` if you want to use it outside of a callback. It's also possible to use methods or functions to get structure with the main propsal's design. Lastly, it would be possible to build a callback-based API like the above using the APIs from the main proposal, but the opposite wouldn't be as simple.

### MapGroup takes a constructed GroupRouteBuilder as a parameter

```C#
var app = WebApplication.Create(args);

var group = new GroupRouteBuilder("/todos");

group.MapGet("/", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.ToListAsync());
group.MapGet("/{id}", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.GetAsync(id));
group.MapPost("/", (Todo todo, TodoDb db) => db.AddAsync(todo));

var nestedGroup = new GroupRouteBuilder("/{org}");
nestedGroup.MapGet("/", (string org, TodoDb db) => db.Filter(todo => todo.Org == org).ToListAsync());
group.MapGroup(nestedGroup).RequireCors("AllowAll");

group.OfBuilder<RouteHandlerBuilder>(builder =>
{
    builder.WithTags("todos");
});

app.MapGroup(group);

// Or, do we prefer to define the route prefix later? This means RequestDelegateFactory would have to
// delay inspecting the final route pattern until the Endpoint is resolved from the EndpointDataSource.
// In the following examples, GroupRouteBuilder would just have a default constructor.
app.MapGroup("/todos", group).RequireCors("AllowAll");
app.MapGroup("/todos2", group).RequireCors("AllowAll");

// OfBuilder would also apply to the following even though it was called earlier.
group.MapDelete("/{id}", (int id, TodoDb db) => db.DeleteAsync(id));

// We'll also have to guard against recursion. The following would need to throw ASAP
//nestedGroup.MapGroup(group);
```

I like this design to, but I think it's less discoverable to have to call `new GroupRouteBuilder(...)` before calling `MapGroup` rather than just having `MapGroup` return the `GroupRouteBuilder`. It's kind of nice to add the same group to multiple prefixes and to not have to supply a group prefix RoutePattern until after all the endpoints have been added to a group, but I'm not sure that's worth the extra step.

## Future

We do want to have `GroupRouteBuilder` implement `IApplicationBuilder` like the original proposal. I hope to add this in a later iteration. Would we be able just remove the prefix from `HttpContext.Request.Path`? We still need to think about how this affects things like link generation.

@halter73 

This is a pretty good start. I'm not sure if there is a lot of benefit to a separate interface, but `GroupRouteBuilder` **must** not be `sealed` and have a `public` constructor if it doesn't not implement it. Even if it does, there should be a really good reason to make it inextensible. As an extender that has bitten me many, many times.

Since the design will be an extension method on `IEndpointRouteBuilder` and it is after DI container construction, this extension method should resolve the instance from the container so that it can be replaced without having to write new, alternate extension methods.

There are several ways this can be accomplished. The top approaches are probably:

1. Just make `GroupPrefix` read/write
2. `internal protected virtual void GroupPrefix(RoutePattern pattern)` so it can be extended
3. an interface with `RoutePattern GroupPrefix { get; set; }`, but an implementation of `RoutePattern GroupPrefix { get; protected set; }`
4. use a factory

I'm all for helping developers fall into the _Pit of Success_, but not at the expense of an API that's too rigid. It's a bit more work, but a factory probably provides the best of both worlds. Something like:

```c#
interface IGroupRouteBuilderFactory
{
    GroupRouteBuilder NewBuilder(IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, RoutePattern pattern);
    GroupRouteBuilder NewBuilder(IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, string pattern);
}
```

You can imagine that the container might setup:

```c#
services.TryAddSingleton<IGroupRouteBuilderFactory, DefaultGroupRouteBuilderFactory>();
```

And then you'd have:

```c#
public static class EndpointRouteBuilderExtensions
{
    public static GroupRouteBuilder MapGroup(this IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, RoutePattern pattern) =>
        endpoints.Services.GetRequiredService<IGroupRouteBuilderFactory>().NewBuilder(endpoints, pattern)

    public static GroupRouteBuilder MapGroup(this IEndpointRouteBuilder endpoints, string pattern) =>
        endpoints.Services.GetRequiredService<IGroupRouteBuilderFactory>().NewBuilder(endpoints, pattern)
}
```

I don't have super strong opinions about _how_ this is achieved. My point and caution is having an extension method that is the only way to create a `GroupRouteBuilder`.

The next thing is that `GroupRouteBuilder` _may_ be missing a needed `IList<object> Metadata { get; }` property to carry forth a monad of metadata bits that can be populated through extension methods. This would be analogous to how metadata is applied to endpoints.

Consider that the API Versioning preview approach looks like:

```c#
app.DefineApi("Orders")
   .HasApiVersion(1.0)
   .HasApiVersion(2.0)
   .ReportApiVersions()
   .HasMapping(api =>
    {
        api.MapGet("/order/{id: int}", (int id) => new Order(id))
           .MapToApiVersion( 1.0 )
           .MapToApiVersion( 2.0 );

        api.MapPost("/order", (HttpRequest request, Order order) =>
            {
                order.Id = 42;
                var scheme = request.Scheme;
                var host = request.host;
                var location = $"{scheme}://{host}/order/{order.Id}";
                return Results.Created(location, order);
            } )
           .MapToApiVersion( 2.0 );

        api.MapDelete("/order/{id: int}", (int id) => Results.NoContent())
           .IsApiVersionNeutral();
    } );
```

Aligning to this design would look something more like:

```c#
var orders = app.MapGroup("order")
                .HasName("Orders")    // ← metadata, but how do we attach it without type-specific extensions?
                .HasApiVersion(1.0)   // ← more metadata    
                .HasApiVersion(2.0)   // ← more metadata
                .ReportApiVersions(); // ← more metadata

orders.MapGet("{id: int}", (int id) => new Order(id))
      .MapToApiVersion( 1.0 )    // ← no current way to apply metadata here either without type replacement
      .MapToApiVersion( 2.0 );

orders.MapPost((HttpRequest request, Order order) =>
       {
           order.Id = 42;
           var scheme = request.Scheme;
           var host = request.host;
           var location = $"{scheme}://{host}/order/{order.Id}";
           return Results.Created(location, order);
       } )
      .MapToApiVersion( 2.0 );

orders.MapDelete("{id: int}", (int id) => Results.NoContent())
      .IsApiVersionNeutral();
```

A possible alternative might look something like:

```c#
app.DefineApi("Orders")
   .HasApiVersion(1.0)
   .HasMapping(api =>
    {
        var orders = api.MapGroup("order");
        api.MapGet("{id: int}", (int id) => new Order(id));
    } );
```

For nested or subgroups, it makes more sense for a group to be a factory for another group. For example:

```c#
var api = app.MapGroup("api");      // ~/api
var orders = api.MapGroup("order"); // ~/api/order
```

This would also eliminate the possibility of recursion. 

I might lose the battle, but I still have to call it out. URLs don't have _groups_. I know it's pedantic, but words matter. The word _"group"_ implies a hierarchy which doesn't exist. Adding `/` into a path is easy for humans to read, but there is no group or hierarchy. The path could have just as easily been a GUID. There is common conflation of URL path with file system path, but that is a false equivalency. File system directories _do_ have hierarchy.

In the spirit of naming, keeping things simple, and retaining parity with the established paradigm, how about `MapRoute`? I didn't see that used anywhere else. This would have it look like:

```c#
var api = app.MapRoute("api")
var orders = api.MapRoute("order");
orders.MapGet("{id:int", (int id) => new Order());
```

This _feels_ closer to what we've been used to with attributes. Agree or disagree, this also has the benefit of not implying group or hierarchy, even if that is someone's mental model.

I don't like `OfBuilder<TBuilder>` at all. It's unclear how it's to be used or what type to ask for. From the example you've shown, it looks like things will get very messy. In my opinion, we need to think about this some more.

Finally, where or how is `IGroupEndpointDataSource` used? Do we even need it? `IEndpointRouteBuilder` already supports having multiple `EndpointDataSources` and those can be coalesced into `CompositeEndpointDataSource`. I'm not sure I'm seeing a need to know or maintain a collection of builders.  Despite being called _builders_ they don't really _build_ in the traditional sense as there is no `Build()` method (which kind of threw me off at first). They add to an `EndpointDataSource` that _builds_ when the endpoints are realized/requested. I guess where I'm going with this is where would you use `IEnumerable<IEndpointConventionBuilder>`? 

Is there a rough timeline as to when there might be some code to look at or play with?

